        "id": 1308504
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-12-07T14:56:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Usually your assumption is correct if Settings is the only caller (Bluetooth tethering button would be gray out till tethering trigger callback). But if OEM have its own application would also call startTethering(TETHEIRNG_BLUETOOTH), then your assumption would be wrong. Here is the case:\nIf bluetooth just shutdown (e.g. crash), mBluetoothPanListener would be reset to null. Then Settings and oem app start bluetooth at the same time. first one would create the listener and call getProfileProxy to bind to bluetooth service. The later one\u0027s request should be enqueue here instead of create new biner to bluetooth service.",
      "parentUuid": "c04cb9a0_94815a2c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 716,
        "startChar": 10,
        "endLine": 716,
        "endChar": 16
      },
      "revId": "40924724fd48e3d2d3f58c4522cd68bfc2bec91e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8ea343f7_f212a660",
        "filename": "Tethering/src/com/android/networkstack/tethering/Tethering.java",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 717,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000835
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-12-07T08:34:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This stores the listener in a persistent member in this class. That raises two questions:\n- The listener no longer goes out of scope (and is no longer garbage collected) once this method returns. Can this cause a memory leak in the app that passed in the listener? This listener will maintain a reference a binder proxy in another process, and maybe that can maintain a reference to something expensive.\n- If the calling process dies, will the listener ever be freed? If not, is that a memory leak in the networkstack process? Or (perhaps even worse) could it make it impossible to stop bluetooth tethering, because there is always a pending request for it?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 717,
        "startChar": 53,
        "endLine": 717,
        "endChar": 61
      },
      "revId": "40924724fd48e3d2d3f58c4522cd68bfc2bec91e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cc562c6d_580bf503",
        "filename": "Tethering/src/com/android/networkstack/tethering/Tethering.java",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 717,
      "author": {
        "id": 1308504
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-12-07T14:56:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The reference should only exist when app want to start tethering but tethering is not bind to bluetooth yet. Once tethering bind to pan service (onServiceConnected) or bluetooth just crash (onServiceDisconnected). The mPendingPanRequests would be cleared and reference doesn\u0027t exist. I think the leak only could happen if onServiceConnected/onServiceDisonnected never be called, and this should be a bug.\n\nIf the calling process dies(e.g. Settings), listener would still not be freed till onServiceConnected (I think tethering should always receive onServiceConnected because Settings would always turn on bluetooth adaptor to bring up bluetooth service (if it is unavailable) before calling startTethering). Once onServiceConnected, Calling a died \"listener\" would be no-op. died \"listener\" would throw RemoteException and we already catch it inside sendTetherResult function.",
      "parentUuid": "8ea343f7_f212a660",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 717,
        "startChar": 53,
        "endLine": 717,
        "endChar": 61
      },
      "revId": "40924724fd48e3d2d3f58c4522cd68bfc2bec91e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "923d3a44_fe3bb399",
        "filename": "Tethering/src/com/android/networkstack/tethering/Tethering.java",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 725,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000835
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-12-07T08:34:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If bluetooth tethering is quickly turned on, then off, then on again, then onServiceConnected (or onServiceDisconnected) could be called on an old listener that is not mBluetoothPanListener.\n\nNormally you\u0027d put something like:\n\n    // Ignore calls to stale listeners.\n    if (this !\u003d mBluetoothPanListener) return;\n\nboth here and in onServiceConnected. But I don\u0027t know whether that would break anything on existing devices. Would it?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 725,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 725,
        "endChar": 37
      },
      "revId": "40924724fd48e3d2d3f58c4522cd68bfc2bec91e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "69b97c7a_88d1df54",
        "filename": "Tethering/src/com/android/networkstack/tethering/Tethering.java",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 725,
      "author": {
        "id": 1308504
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-12-07T14:56:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We create new ServiceListener only when old ServiceListener is onServiceDisconnected (mBluetoothPanListener is set to null). onServiceDisconnected means bluetooth process is gone and we also close the profile proxy to prevent the old service auto reconnect when bluetooth process is back. So the calls to stale listeners seems unlikely. In case the bug in bluetooth side, the suggestion looks safer. Done.",
      "parentUuid": "923d3a44_fe3bb399",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 725,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 725,
        "endChar": 37
      },
